update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('f262bbf4-d5ee-4580-9eab-e4cc9e432d31'::uuid, 'The five-kingdom system provides a way to organise the wide range of living organisms into broad groups. Start by looking at the features biologists compare, including cell type, level of organisation and how organisms obtain food. This overview prepares you to examine each kingdom and understand why its members are placed together.'),
('fb82bc32-2c82-42c4-ad45-7e17346d27d5'::uuid, 'One of the groups in the traditional five-kingdom classification is Monera. Organisms in this kingdom are generally single-celled and have cells without a membrane-bound nucleus. This section examines their basic organisation, how they obtain nutrients and reproduce, and the roles they play in natural environments and human life.'),
('0745f5dd-6b15-4489-990f-b32821c47c78'::uuid, 'Having considered organisms with simpler cell organisation, turn to Protoctista, a diverse group that includes many microscopic eukaryotes. This section explores their cell structure, habitats and ways of obtaining food. You will see why organisms placed in this kingdom do not all share the same appearance or way of life.'),
('a8d35430-be72-4931-b3e0-3b5a05860098'::uuid, 'Fungi have a distinctive way of obtaining nutrients: they absorb dissolved substances from their surroundings. This section examines their body organisation, feeding and reproduction, and considers examples such as moulds, mushrooms and yeasts. You will connect these features with the useful and harmful effects fungi can have.'),
('a83c940e-53b1-4b57-a26f-38aa498d5b1d'::uuid, 'Plants are generally multicellular organisms whose cells contain cellulose cell walls, and most make organic food through photosynthesis. This section examines the features used to recognise members of Plantae and considers how their structures support nutrition, growth and reproduction. It builds on your earlier study of plant organs and their functions.'),
('626839dd-0a2f-4888-ade8-21a4d8848924'::uuid, 'Animals are multicellular organisms that obtain nutrients by feeding on other organisms or organic matter. This section examines the features that distinguish Animalia, including their cell organisation, feeding and movement. You will also consider the range of body forms found in the kingdom and how animals are adapted to different ways of life.'),
('74b020a2-43d1-4267-9f60-1aa90e27a4c6'::uuid, 'With the purpose of classification in mind, begin by comparing the broad features used to distinguish the five kingdoms. This theme brings together cell organisation, nutrition and body structure, giving you a framework for understanding why organisms are placed in different groups.'),
('90682c66-f514-44f9-b377-68ebfbcaabd6'::uuid, 'The overview of the five kingdoms introduces the features used to compare living organisms. Now apply those ideas to Monera, focusing on the organisation of its cells, the ways its members obtain nutrients and their importance in the environment.'),
('151fbd41-aa60-4d28-befe-27b15fb8a738'::uuid, 'The five-kingdom comparison includes organisms whose cells have a true nucleus but whose organisation differs from plants, animals and fungi. This theme focuses on Protoctista and the variety of forms, habitats and nutritional methods found among its members.'),
('2ea6bb36-254e-4a49-a522-e1c83073d067'::uuid, 'After comparing Monera and Protoctista, examine a group with a different method of nutrition. This theme focuses on the structure and activities of fungi, particularly how they obtain food by absorption and how they reproduce.'),
('90ea1a87-83d3-45b5-b694-b0714a5e575a'::uuid, 'The features used to classify plants become clearer when you consider how their cells and bodies are organised. This theme focuses on the characteristics of Plantae and how plant structures support photosynthesis, transport and reproduction.'),
('9df6dcdd-cf04-4d29-a3ae-ccf61aec0452'::uuid, 'Complete the comparison by examining animals, whose multicellular bodies are organised for feeding, movement and response to their surroundings. This theme introduces the defining features of Animalia and prepares you to recognise the diversity within the kingdom.'),
('64dfc486-88b3-4595-8bf8-dc06af64b510'::uuid, 'The five-kingdom classification groups organisms according to important differences in their cells and ways of life. In this lesson, you will identify the five groups used in this system and compare them by cell type, organisation and nutrition. You will learn how these features provide a basis for classifying organisms.'),
('6d8cc77f-f0b0-4960-b62d-863814b5a269'::uuid, 'Monera includes organisms whose cells lack a membrane-bound nucleus. In this lesson, you will describe their basic cell organisation, identify common examples such as bacteria and explain how they obtain nutrients and reproduce. You will also consider their roles in decomposition, nutrient cycling, health and disease.'),
('acddf4ce-8d06-4757-9b40-b66ef37efd52'::uuid, 'Protoctista includes a varied collection of mostly simple eukaryotic organisms, many of which live in water or moist environments. In this lesson, you will describe their general cell organisation and explore examples such as Amoeba, Paramecium and some algae. You will compare how members obtain nutrients and move, recognising that the kingdom contains diverse forms.'),
('79728298-b908-489c-813f-f917ba9c4834'::uuid, 'Fungi are eukaryotic organisms that absorb nutrients from organic material. In this lesson, you will identify their main features, including hyphae and mycelium in many forms, and explain how they feed and reproduce. You will use examples such as yeast, moulds and mushrooms to distinguish fungi from plants and animals.'),
('7640582e-dfdd-4ecf-8cad-ebfab5fba7b0'::uuid, 'Members of Plantae share features that support a largely photosynthetic way of life. In this lesson, you will describe the main characteristics of plants, including multicellular organisation and cellulose cell walls, and explain the role of chloroplasts in photosynthesis. You will relate these features to the way plants obtain and use resources.'),
('8b90b731-9736-4283-817a-242f2acb3438'::uuid, 'Animals show a wide range of body forms, but they share important features that distinguish them from the other kingdoms. In this lesson, you will describe the general characteristics of Animalia, including multicellular organisation, feeding and the absence of cell walls. You will consider how movement and specialised tissues support animal life.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and (c.description is distinct from v.description);
